THE BUG
main() in scripts/glue_cdc_continuous.py raised
  Exception("No usable tables from the manifest.")
when `not contexts and not multi_key`, IGNORING not_owned. A task whose tables are ALL
owned by fork jobs (e.g. one big table whose bg-<slug> fork owns it after a
partitions-auto source wrote many LOAD files) therefore crashed the MAIN CDC run on
startup. In stepfunctions/startup.asl.json the main run must be alive (IsCdcRunAlive)
and have written its _cdc_started marker (CheckCdcStarted) BEFORE StartForkCdcMap starts
the forks. So the crash routed to CdcRunFailed / CdcStartNotConfirmed, the owning fork
never started, and the task got NO CDC at all.

THE FIX
- Raise ONLY when nothing is usable: `not contexts and not multi_key and not not_owned`.
- When every table is owned by another CDC job, take the EXISTING all-multi-key idle
  path: log it clearly (list the tables), write the start marker (task-level + own, as
  today), and stay idle in the poll loop until cutover's BatchStopJobRun stops it. No
  other behavior change.
- Verified the idle path reaches write_started_marker() (the key CheckCdcStarted polls),
  so IsCdcStarted passes and StartForkCdcMap starts the owning fork.

SCOPE
- cdc.json AND cdc-spark.json both run this same script -> one fix covers Python-shell
  and Spark CDC.
- glue_cdc_composite.py already idles on `not contexts` (never raised) — no bug.
- drain_check / cutover / stop_cdc_run are ownership-agnostic — no assumption that the
  main job owns a table — no bug.

